## Part 3 — Hypothesis Testing (notebooks/02_hypotheses.ipynb)

Each hypothesis gets its own section with: the null hypothesis stated clearly, the test/chart, and a written conclusion.

### H1 — Age effect on pass rate

**Null hypothesis:** There is no significant difference in first-time pass rates between vehicles under and over 10 years old.

**Test:**
- Line chart: first-time pass rate (%) vs vehicle age (0–30 years), with a vertical line at 10 years
- Chi-square test comparing pass rates for vehicles ≤10 years vs >10 years
- Bonus: logistic regression curve showing probability of passing as a function of age

### H2 — Brand reliability

**Null hypothesis:** Premium brands (BMW, Mercedes-Benz, Audi, Volvo, Lexus) do not have higher pass rates than budget brands (Dacia, Chevrolet, Daewoo, Lada) when controlling for vehicle age.

**Test:**
- Bar chart: pass rate per brand (top 30 brands by volume), sorted
- Age-controlled comparison: compute pass rate for 5–15 year old vehicles only (controlling for age), compare premium vs budget segment
- Mann-Whitney U test for statistical significance

**Premium brands:** BMW, MERCEDES-BENZ, AUDI, VOLVO, LEXUS, PORSCHE, JAGUAR, LAND ROVER
**Budget brands:** DACIA, CHEVROLET, DAEWOO, LADA, SEAT, KIA, HYUNDAI (adjust based on actual data)

### H3 — Station strictness vs volume

**Null hypothesis:** There is no correlation between inspection station volume (number of inspections per year) and fail rate.

**Test:**
- Scatter plot: station volume (x) vs fail rate % (y), one dot per station, sized by volume
- Pearson/Spearman correlation coefficient
- Map visualisation if station location data is available (it likely isn't in this dataset, but check)

